title="Johnson He">Johnson He</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter><fr:p>While reading <fr:link
type="local"
href="von-neumann-1925.xml"
addr="von-neumann-1925"
title="An Axiomatization of Set Theory">An Axiomatization of Set Theory</fr:link>, I just had the completely trivial-- and
    "that's… the point"-- "realization" that first order logic is the
    meta-language of axiomatic mathematics, just as (it seems) logical
    frameworks are the meta-languages of (typically λ-calculus based)
    programming languages. Well not literally, but the discrepancy is
    non-substantial.</fr:p><fr:p>That is, (classical) FoL is often the setting in which axiomatic
    mathematical theories are formalized. Classic (no pun intended) examples
    are <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[(\mathcal {L}_{group} = \{e, ⋅, -⁻¹\}, Th(\textsf {groups}))]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[(\mathcal {L}_{DLO} = \{≤\}, Th(\textsf {Dense Linear Orders}))]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[(\mathcal {L}_{sets} = \{∈\}, ZFC)]]></fr:tex>.
    (To be ultra-pedantic is a fruitless way, I've paired the language and theory.)</fr:p><fr:p>"definite property"</fr:p></fr:mainmatter><fr:backmatter><fr:tree
